# Architecture

```
src/
  main.rs              # thin entrypoint
  lib.rs               # module root and public re-exports
  presentation/        # cli.rs — argv, file open, exit codes
  application/         # process_transactions.rs — AppError, run(source, sink)
  domain/              # transaction, account, ledger — all business rules
  infrastructure/
    transactions/      # TransactionSource port; csv/ — CsvTransactionReader
    accounts/          # AccountSink port; csv/ — CsvAccountWriter
```

## Dependency rule

```
presentation → application → domain
presentation → infrastructure → domain
application → infrastructure   (trait bounds in run only)
```

- **Domain** depends on nothing external (no `std::io`, no `serde`, no `csv`)
- **Application** orchestrates `run(source, sink)`; depends on domain and infrastructure port traits
- **Infrastructure** defines ports in `transactions/mod.rs` and `accounts/mod.rs`; CSV adapters impl them under `csv/`
- **Presentation** wires CLI to application and constructs infrastructure adapters

## Data flow

1. CLI parses `argv[1]` as input path
2. `CsvTransactionReader` streams parsed `Transaction` rows via `TransactionSource`
3. `run` applies each row to `Ledger`
4. On EOF, `Ledger` produces account snapshots
5. `CsvAccountWriter` serializes snapshots to stdout via `AccountSink`

## Anti-patterns

```rust
// ❌ Domain importing I/O or serde
use csv::Reader;
use serde::Deserialize;

// ❌ Application importing concrete CSV adapters
use crate::infrastructure::transactions::CsvTransactionReader;

// ❌ Business rules in presentation or infrastructure
fn parse_and_apply_deposit(row: &str) { /* balance logic here */ }

// ✅ Domain pure logic; infrastructure adapts I/O
ledger.apply(&transaction);
```
